WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:04.760
Hello.

2
00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:10.960
I'm a professor of psychiatry at Seoul
National University Bundang Hospital.

3
00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:16.260
In this fifth episode, we'll look at
why mood disorders must be treated,

4
00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:20.210
and how recovery unfolds once treatment begins.

5
00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.160
A mood disorder must be treated.

6
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:29.220
Without treatment, controlling your
mood becomes harder and harder.

7
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:35.460
Depression can sometimes get better on its
own, but most people become depressed again.

8
00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:39.670
With each relapse, episodes come
more often and last longer.

9
00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:45.770
In the end, if you don't treat it now, you
will need longer and harder treatment later.

10
00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:52.560
At first, mood shifts with events,
such as a promotion or a breakup.

11
00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:59.310
But if it is left untreated, mood swings
get worse, even without any special event.

12
00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:01.730
First, treatment becomes harder.

13
00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:06.510
The longer and more severe the
illness, the longer treatment takes.

14
00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:12.450
Even a medicine that worked well at first
can lose its effect after repeated relapses.

15
00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:15.530
Second, relationships suffer.

16
00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:18.530
When depressed, you are easily hurt.

17
00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:22.630
When elated, you push yourself forward too much.

18
00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:26.390
Others may mistake you for lazy or fickle.

19
00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:29.910
Third, thinking and memory can be affected.

20
00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:36.850
The more often you relapse, and the longer
you are ill, the more your memory may decline.

21
00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:43.270
Contrary to a common misunderstanding, it
is depression itself, not antidepressants,

22
00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:46.130
that is a risk factor for dementia.

23
00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:49.690
Treating depression actually helps prevent dementia.

24
00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:55.140
Even after treatment starts,
symptoms don't vanish overnight.

25
00:01:55.160 --> 00:02:00.360
They built up over a long time, so
they improve steadily, bit by bit.

26
00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:03.840
Take someone with mood swings as an example.

27
00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:07.300
It takes two to three weeks on medication for changes to

28
00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.360
happen in the brain and for you to start feeling better.

29
00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:15.550
So in the first two to three
weeks, many people feel no effect.

30
00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:19.570
After two to three months, symptoms improve noticeably.

31
00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.670
The range of mood narrows and settles.

32
00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:27.390
At this point, you may actually
feel unmotivated or down.

33
00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:32.870
By six to nine months, you are much more
stable, though mild symptoms may remain.

34
00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:37.400
You may still struggle at times,
but not as severely as before.

35
00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:43.980
And after one to two years, you recover to the
point where you feel you have gotten better.

36
00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:50.870
You may go through a few relapses during
treatment, but they become less frequent.

37
00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:56.350
Even when you feel depressed, it is
milder than before, and passes sooner.

38
00:02:56.570 --> 00:03:03.970
As mood changes ease, your feelings may
seem dulled, and you may feel unmotivated.

39
00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:08.370
As you get used to calmer feelings
and adjust your medication over time,

40
00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:10.550
you will feel more and more comfortable.

41
00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:15.430
If you stay patient and don't give
up, you can definitely get better.

42
00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.180
A state without symptoms is called remission.

43
00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.770
To decide how long to continue treatment,
you must first reach remission.

44
00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:26.970
If you stop treatment before remission,

45
00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:33.430
or stop right after remission without maintenance
treatment, the illness doesn't clear up,

46
00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:36.710
and you end up taking medication continuously.

47
00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:42.830
On the other hand, if you finish the
maintenance period safely, without symptoms,

48
00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:47.050
a time comes when you can stop
medication and stay healthy.

49
00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:52.764
Without maintenance treatment, in
depression and bipolar I disorder,

50
00:03:52.784 --> 00:03:56.250
the chance of relapse within one year is

51
00:03:56.470 --> 00:04:02.390
about fifty percent after a first episode,
about eighty percent after a second,

52
00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:06.110
and about ninety percent after a third or more.

53
00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:12.290
So the usual maintenance period to prevent
relapse is one year after a first episode,

54
00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:17.230
three years after a second, and
lifelong after a third or more.

55
00:04:17.450 --> 00:04:23.590
The actual length is decided together with your
doctor, considering how severe the illness was,

56
00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:28.570
how often it relapsed, whether it was
dangerous, whether it started early,

57
00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:31.890
and whether it runs in your family.

58
00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:35.780
Can you relapse even while taking medication?

59
00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:39.700
Yes, until you have been treated enough, you can.

60
00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:45.640
But with proper treatment, relapses
become fewer, shorter and milder.

61
00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:49.960
If you handle them well, your
overall course may even improve.

62
00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:52.360
So rather than worrying about relapse,

63
00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:57.000
it is important to know the early
symptoms before a relapse fully begins.

64
00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:59.980
These are called prodromal symptoms.

65
00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:05.780
Early in mania or hypomania, you
feel good or become more irritable,

66
00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:10.700
speech and thoughts speed up, you
don't feel tired even with less sleep,

67
00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:13.340
and plans and spending increase.

68
00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:20.500
Early in depression, nothing seems enjoyable,
you feel drained, speech and thoughts slow down,

69
00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:25.680
sleep and appetite change a lot,
and pessimistic thoughts increase.

70
00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:29.080
Prodromal symptoms differ from person to person.

71
00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:35.930
Keeping a mood chart regularly is the best way to
notice your own relapse signs early and respond.

72
00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:41.960
A mood disorder must be treated,
and recovery comes slowly.

73
00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:47.350
Even after you feel better, maintenance
treatment protects you from relapse.

74
00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:51.790
Next time, we'll look at the kinds
of medicine used for mood disorders,

75
00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:54.290
and the side effects you should know about.

76
00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:55.410
Thank you.
